## Target discreteness and ties

[DERIVED] For `lost ∈ {0,1,2,3}`, the normalized declared target remains
`forecast_surplus_bits / kappa − lost`. The Q2 schema change cannot alter it,
and [RUN] row-for-row target equality was exact.

| exact absorbing floor | rows | fraction of all rows | fraction within lost class |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| `0` | 0 | 0.0000% | not observed |
| `−1` | 300 | 1.5270% | 11.6595% |
| `−2` | 400 | 2.0359% | 11.4646% |
| `−3` | 4,909 | 24.9860% | 36.1354% |

[RUN] Lost-offset classes contain 0 / 2,573 / 3,489 / 13,585 rows for 0 / 1 /
2 / 3 lost offsets: `0.0000%`, `13.0961%`, `17.7584%`, and `69.1454%`.
The target mode is the `−3` floor, but there are 13,903 exact target values
over `−33.0198` to `14.5196`.

[RUN] Ties are material but do not dominate ordering: 17,041 of 86,823
within-group pairs (`19.6273%`) are true ties and excluded, leaving 69,782
non-tied pairs. Only `10.4268%` of non-tied pairs cross lost-offset classes;
20% of groups have a tied true maximum.

[RUN] A predictor emitting only modal floor `−3` has ordering `0.5000`, top-1
`0.2450`, and `R² −0.2735`: no skill. The richer exposed absorbing score
`−lost` has ordering `0.5439`, top-1 exactly `0.2450`, and `R² 0.0301`, while
tying on `89.5732%` of non-tied pairs and being exactly chance within each
lost class. Floor prediction is not interpreted as skill.

